<Base title="Planning a Business Website: A Practical Brief" description="Before choosing colours or animations, decide who the website is for and what a useful visit looks like."><article class="prose wrap"><a class="eyebrow" href="/insights">INSIGHTS / PRACTICAL THINKING</a><h1>What should a business website actually do?</h1><p class="article-lead">Before choosing colours or animations, decide who the website is for and what a useful visit looks like.</p><h2>Answer the first questions</h2><p>What do you offer? Who is it for? Where do you work? Why should someone trust you? Use language customers recognise rather than broad promises.</p><h2>Give services their own space</h2><p>Help visitors find relevant explanations, examples and next steps. Each page should add useful detail, rather than repeating the same text with a different town name.</p><h2>Show work you can explain</h2><p>Describe the problem, your contribution and the result you can substantiate. Distinguish client work from demonstrations and avoid numbers you cannot support.</p><h2>Make enquiry straightforward</h2><p>Ask for enough information to start a conversation. Test the form, make errors understandable and provide an email or phone alternative.</p><h2>Give search engines clarity</h2><p>Useful page titles, crawlable links, canonical URLs and a sitemap help search engines understand your pages. Fast delivery and good mobile layouts help visitors. None guarantees a ranking.</p><p>Explore our <a href="/consultancy">consultancy</a> and <a href="/software-development-northern-ireland">software development services</a>, or <a href="/contact">tell us what you have in mind</a>.</p><p class="article-byline">By <a href="https://www.glenadamson.co.uk/about">Glen Adamson</a>, founder of Modern Software.</p></article><CTA/></Base>
